+# Team Caplet working notes (Google Doc snapshot, 2026-09-27)
+Source: "2026 Wharton Global High School Investment Competition" Google Doc (team notes). Student-authored brainstorming; not official material.
+
+## INVESTMENT PHILOSOPHY
+The case is functionally a defined-benefit pension-funding problem from 2033: 10 x $50,000 fixed nominal payments (2033-2042) = fixed nominal annuity liability that must be funded with a high degree of certainty. Therefore use Liability-Driven Investing (LDI).
+
+### LDI toolkit
+- DEDICATION (cash-flow matching): buy Treasuries maturing when each payment is due (2033...2042), eliminating market risk.
+- IMMUNISATION (duration matching): heading only, not yet written.
+
+## ASSET ALLOCATION / Framework
+### 2 clocks
+1. Growth clock (2027-2031): no withdrawals; "full license" for growth-tilted, equity-heavy portfolio.
+2. Liability clock (2031-2033+): from 2031 (Year 5, co-sponsor conversations start) de-risk toward 2033.
+### 3 buckets (inspired by Ashvin Chhabra's Wealth Allocation Framework)
+1. Personal risk bucket = operating reserve (capital preservation only).
+2. Market risk bucket = facility contribution (reasonable risk).
+3. Aspirational risk bucket = flexibility reserve (stays invested for growth; dry powder).
+### Portfolio design
+- Growth sleeve (2027-2031): diversified global equities with some factor tilt; full risk budget.
+- Glide path "Residency Runway" (2031-2033): equity steps down from ~75% in 2027 to 20-25% by eve of 2033.
+- Operating reserve "Payment Ladder" (from 2033): cash-flow-matched Treasury ladder, one maturity before each of 10 payment dates; defeasance at individual level; "high degree of certainty" = government credit, duration-matched.
+- Tiering (latest version): near payments 2033-2036 locked into actual T-bills/notes/bonds in 2033; 2037-2042 sit in a short-duration bond sleeve and are rolled into the ladder at least 3 years before each payment, to avoid locking today's curve.
+
+## TRADING NOTES: Entry 0 pre-mortem (2026-09-27)
+Risks to avoid: (1) abandoning the growth clock early after a volatile week; (2) chasing a "storyteller"/creator-economy theme for narrative rather than mandate fit; (3) treating WInS as a performance contest (WInS P&L is not added to Laura's projections); (4) reacting to single data points like one day's yield move - de-risking starts 2031 regardless; (5) letting good early results loosen risk discipline ("Certainty Cushion", "Flexibility Retention Factor").
+Decision log columns: date, market signal, hypothesis, action, outcome & lesson.
+
+## CLIENT SECTION (team analysis)
+- "This isn't really a stock-picking case. It's a liability-matching problem with a growth portfolio attached."
+- Why Wharton picked Laura: Wharton Statistics grad (will detect fake confidence intervals; "Monte Carlo 95%" without defined model loses points); storyteller who needs co-sponsors to believe her numbers; lumpy income; goal is a project not retirement - generic growth portfolios are punished.
+- Numbers (at 4%): reserve cost at 2033 L2033 ~ $422k (annuity-due of 10 x $50k); at 3% ~ $439k. Projected 2033 portfolio ~ $626k -> surplus ~ $204k; a 20% drawdown to ~$501k cuts surplus to ~$79k (-61%). Reserve PV in 2027 = L2033/1.04^6 ~ $333k (> first $300k deposit). Risk-free floor for the facility contribution ~ $140k.
+- Rate risk hides in the reserve; teams that wait until 2033 carry equity + rate risk. Lock in early with zero-coupon Treasuries 2033-2042.
+- 2031 promise becomes a second commitment: range should be a rule based on 2031 portfolio value; the floor of the communicated range should be moved into bonds maturing 2033 so it can't be broken.
+- Currency: residency in Taiwan, facility likely paid in TWD - FX risk not excluded by the case. Facility cost inflation (Taiwan ~1.8-2.2%, unverified).
+- "Professionals": protect the promise first, then grow the rest (LDI; private-bank safe/growth buckets).
+- Claim: 10-year Treasury ~5.1% (highest since late 2023) -> locking all ten payments costs ~ $297k at start of 2027 (UNVERIFIED; also should use STRIPS zero yields per maturity, not 10y par).
+- Options: lock everything early / lock part now and raise it by rules (author leans to this) / wait until 2033.
+- "High degree of certainty" should mean ~100% (pension standard), not the 80-90% planners accept for adjustable goals.
+- In 2031 promise a floor then protect it; List & Lucking-Reiley (2002) seed-money experiment: raising seed from 10% to 67% of goal gave ~6x more giving.
+- Stress the 2028 $150k contribution being late or smaller.
+- Laura: born Wuhan, raised Texas; The Wuhan I Know, Messy Roots; Wharton stats; PM in tech; entrepreneurial; "money for a dream, not money for survival"; fears breaking a promise and losing credibility; wants rigorous but explainable plan.
+
+## Money: two jobs
+Job 1 the promise ($500k, near-certain). Job 2 the dream (largest responsible facility contribution, variable).
+
+## Random notes
+- "We must be invested in at least 6 unique sectors at any one time" (UNVERIFIED; third-party sources say sector minimum = number of team members; team has 6).
+
+## KNOWN INTERNAL CONTRADICTIONS (flagged for the team)
+1. Lock-in timing: Client section says lock (part of) the reserve early; Philosophy section says buy 2033-2036 only in 2033 and keep 2037-2042 short-duration until >=3y before - which re-exposes the reserve to falling rates.
+2. Risk budget: "full risk budget, ~75% equities" in the growth clock vs. the finding that the reserve PV (~$297-333k) is about the whole first deposit, leaving little room for risk.
+3. Rate assumption inconsistency: 4% in one place, 5.1% in another.
